<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Privacy Policy &middot; Family Assistant</title>
<meta name="robots" content="index, follow">
<style>
  :root {
    color-scheme: light dark;
    --bg: #ffffff;
    --fg: #1c1c1e;
    --muted: #55555c;
    --rule: #e0e0e4;
    --accent: #0b5fbd;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root {
      --bg: #16171a;
      --fg: #ececf1;
      --muted: #a5a5ae;
      --rule: #33343a;
      --accent: #7fb2ff;
    }
  }
  * { box-sizing: border-box; }
  body {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 2.5rem 1.25rem 4rem;
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--fg);
    font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    line-height: 1.6;
    font-size: 17px;
  }
  main { max-width: 44rem; margin: 0 auto; }
  h1 { font-size: 1.9rem; line-height: 1.25; margin: 0 0 0.25rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.25rem; margin: 2.25rem 0 0.5rem; }
  h3 { font-size: 1.05rem; margin: 1.5rem 0 0.35rem; }
  p, li { color: var(--fg); }
  ul { padding-left: 1.25rem; }
  li { margin: 0.3rem 0; }
  a { color: var(--accent); }
  .meta { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }
  .summary {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg) 4%, transparent);
  }
  .summary p:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .summary p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  footer {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<main>
  <h1>Family Assistant Privacy Policy</h1>
  <p class="meta">Last updated: {{ last_updated }}</p>

  <div class="summary">
    <p><strong>In short:</strong> Family Assistant is self-hosted software. The app talks only to the
    server address you enter when you set it up. There is no Family Assistant company, no central
    service, no analytics or advertising SDKs, and no sale or sharing of your data for advertising.
    Your information lives on the server you (or the person who invited you) run, and is processed
    by the AI providers and integrations that server is configured to use.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Who this policy covers</h2>
  <p>This policy describes how the Family Assistant iOS app, web interface and backend server handle
  personal information. This copy of the policy is published by the operator of this particular
  instance: {{ operator }}. That operator is the data controller for everything stored in it.</p>
  <p>Family Assistant is intended for a household and the people it invites. The app is only usable
  against an instance you have been given access to; who may reach that instance, and how they sign
  in, is configured by its operator.</p>

  <h2>What the app collects</h2>
  <p>The iOS app collects no information for its own purposes. It has no analytics, advertising,
  attribution or tracking SDKs, and it does not build a profile of you. Everything below is
  information you send to <em>your</em> server so the assistant can do what you asked.</p>

  <h3>Information you provide</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Conversations</strong> — the messages you send to the assistant and its replies,
    including anything you paste or dictate into them.</li>
    <li><strong>Content you create</strong> — notes, tasks, reminders, calendar events, automations
    and similar records.</li>
    <li><strong>Attachments and documents</strong> — photos, PDFs, text files, audio and other files
    you upload or share into the app.</li>
    <li><strong>Voice input</strong> — when you start a voice conversation, microphone audio is
    streamed for as long as that conversation is active. The app requests microphone access only for
    this, and only when you use it.</li>
    <li><strong>Connected accounts</strong> — if you link an integration such as Google (Gmail,
    Calendar, Drive), Telegram, email intake or a smart-home system, the credentials for that link
    and the data it retrieves are stored on your server.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Information collected automatically</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Account and session data</strong> — your sign-in identity, session cookies and API
    tokens issued to your devices.</li>
    <li><strong>Push notification tokens</strong> — an Apple Push Notification service device token,
    if you enable notifications, so the server can notify that device.</li>
    <li><strong>Diagnostics</strong> — error reports and short-lived technical breadcrumbs (such as
    connection events and app-version information) sent to your own server to help debug problems.
    These are stored on your server, not sent to any third-party crash or analytics service.</li>
    <li><strong>Server logs</strong> — ordinary request logs kept by the server and its hosting
    infrastructure.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The app does not collect location, contacts, health data, photos you have not chosen to share,
  or an advertising identifier.</p>

  <h2>How the information is used</h2>
  <p>Solely to provide the assistant: answering your messages, carrying out tasks you ask for,
  storing and retrieving your notes, documents, calendar and reminders, sending notifications you
  have enabled, and diagnosing faults. It is not used for advertising, profiling, or training
  third-party AI models — see below.</p>

  <h2>Who the information is shared with</h2>
  <p>Your data is not sold, and is not shared for advertising or marketing. It is disclosed only to
  the services your instance is configured to use:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>AI model providers</strong> — the content of your conversations, and the data the
    assistant needs to answer them, is sent to the model provider(s) configured on your server (for
    example OpenAI, Google or Anthropic) for processing. Their handling of that data is governed by
    the API terms and privacy policies of whichever providers your operator has configured; these
    APIs are used under terms that do not permit training on submitted content.</li>
    <li><strong>Apple</strong> — push notification payloads pass through the Apple Push Notification
    service in order to reach your device. TestFlight builds also report crashes to Apple under
    <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/">Apple's privacy policy</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Integrations you connect</strong> — services such as Google, Telegram, your email
    provider or a smart-home system, but only for the data needed to fulfil the request.</li>
    <li><strong>Hosting infrastructure</strong> — whoever hosts the server (which may be the
    operator's own hardware).</li>
    <li><strong>Legal obligations</strong> — the operator may disclose data if legally required to.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Storage, retention and security</h2>
  <p>Data is stored in the database of the instance you use, under the operator's control, and is
  kept until you or the operator delete it. Traffic between the app and the server is encrypted with
  HTTPS, and device sessions can be revoked at any time from the server's token settings. No system
  is perfectly secure, and this is self-hosted software: the security of your data depends on how
  the operator configures and maintains their server, including how they restrict access to it.</p>

  <h2>Your choices and rights</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Delete notes, calendar events, automations and attachments at any time, either in the app or
    by asking the assistant to do it. For anything the app has no delete control for — conversation
    history, uploaded documents — ask the operator, who can remove it from the server.</li>
    <li>Turn off notifications in iOS Settings, and revoke a device's access from the app's token
    settings.</li>
    <li>Disconnect any linked account to stop further access to it.</li>
    <li>Ask the operator for a copy of your data, for its correction, or for its deletion, including
    the deletion of your whole account. Depending on where you live (for example under the GDPR or
    the CCPA) you may have a legal right to these; the operator honours such requests.</li>
    <li>Deleting the app removes local caches from your device but not data already stored on the
    server — ask the operator to delete the account for that.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>Family Assistant is designed for household use, and a household may include children whose
  parent or guardian sets up and supervises their access. The software is not directed at children
  generally, is not distributed to the public, and no data is collected from anyone who has not been
  invited to an instance by its operator. Where a child uses the assistant, the parent or guardian
  operating the instance controls that child's data and can delete it at any time.</p>

  <h2>International use</h2>
  <p>Your data is stored wherever the operator hosts the server, and may be processed in other
  countries by the AI providers and integrations described above.</p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>If this policy changes, the updated version is published at this address with a new "last
  updated" date. Material changes will also be communicated by the operator.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  {% if contact_email %}
  <p>Questions, or requests about your data: <a href="mailto:{{ contact_email }}">{{ contact_email }}</a>.</p>
  {% else %}
  <p>Questions, or requests about your data, should go to the operator of this instance, who invited
  you to it.</p>
  {% endif %}

  <footer>
    Family Assistant is self-hosted software. This page is served by the instance you are viewing and
    describes that instance.
  </footer>
</main>
</body>
</html>
